Good evening,

Do not read past here, unless you are willing to comply with everything I
say :-)

This message optimiser is still not 100% tested, so anyone who grabs this
file, uses it and has problems is completely on their own!!!

I've put it up here, as it SEEMS to be working fairly reliably, and I need
a larger testing group, ie a horde of guinea pigs ;-)

OK firstly, I basically have to warn you, that this proggy could
conceivably do all sorts of nasty things to your BBS files and/or your HDisk.

So as much as I regret this, you're are going to have to take full
responsibility for anything that happens, sorry :-(

OK now that I've wimped out of all responsibility ;-), I'll tell ya' how to

Firstly please make a backup copy of your BBS:messages files, and always
have a recent backup of them, whenever using my Optimiser, that way if
anything ever goes wrong, you won't totally lose your message base. Also
this sort of program will take at least a few weeks of constant use, before
it can even be considered reliable!

*NOTE* This proggy dosen't yet notify MAXS of a new message.index.
therefore after having run the optimiser, and copied the optimised files to
BBS:messages/ You must exit MAXS, and restart it. This is because MAXS will
still contain the old version of the message.index file. If you do not restart
MAXS after an optimisation, then there will be lots of probs from combining an
unoptimised message.index with an optimised message.data and optimised
message.text!

Usage:  Optimiser PATH1 PATH2 -V

PATH1 : Compulsory, the source path of the unoptimised message files.  eg.
        BBS:messages/

PATH2 : Compulsory, the destination path of the optimised messages. eg. RAM: etc
*NOTE* PATH1 can be the same as PATH2

     optimises, note this option requires you to hit a key after each message,
     and is therefore definatley not recommended on any message base that has
     more than a hundred or so messages. What's it for? Good question, I'll get
     back to ya's on that :-)

If exactly two/three args are not entered, then the optimiser will not do
anything.

It takes the three files messages.data, messages.index and messages.text
from PATH1 and produces the files messages.data.opt, messages.index.opt and
messages.text.opt in PATH2. The reason I did it this way, is for added
safety, the original messages are basically gauranteed to be unmodified.

It works with already optimised files, and empty message bases, giving
appropriate warnings!

That's about all I can think off, any questions/critisisms/comments/bugs,
then please let me know!

One more thing, if you have a temp: partition or some such, please use that
as the destination PATH for the optimiser, ie PATH2. That way if anything
goes wrong, you will only lose that partition!

And remember, after optimising and copying the new files over, exit MAXS


